entry.summary:
Log-To-Leak: Prompt Injection Attacks on Tool-Using LLM Agents via the Model Context Protocol
arXiv · Oct 27, 2026This paper studies security risks for LLM agents that access external tools through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). It introduces Log-To-Leak, an attack that makes an agent place an extra, attacker-controlled tool call after the legitimate one, leaking the user query, server name, server response, and agent response. Across 18 valid model-server pairs, the attack reaches an attack success rate of 62.64% to 100%, and three tested defenses still leave over 55% residual success on GitHub MCP with GPT-4o. The authors conclude that a task can finish correctly while unauthorized disclosure happens, and they recommend host-controlled authorization for secondary tool calls.}
★★★★ Notable: The authors propose Log-To-Leak, which on the known MCP tool-poisoning surface induces an extra attacker-controlled call after the final legitimate call, reporting 62.64-100% attack success across 18
